+# LIFE-626: the codex-cli 0.162.1 trust chooser ("Folder access" screen family).
+#
+# Live captures (LIFE-603 final gate g31, coordinator x06, review r5 r05/r09, review r6 r03/r17):
+#     Folder access                         Folder access
+#     <cwd>                                 <cwd>
+#
+#     Note: You’re in a subdirectory ...    Config, hooks, and exec policies from untrusted
+#     <repo root>                           folders stay disabled. ...
+#
+#     Trust this folder? Codex can ...
+#
+#   › 1. Trust and continue               › 1. Open restricted
+#     2. Back to Agent Command Center       2. Quit
+#
+#     enter continue · esc back             enter continue · esc quit
+#
+# The second screen is what codex shows for a folder it has recorded as untrusted. The binary also
+# carries `Open existing task` / `Keep current directory` labels for the same screen, so the rule
+# matches the family (LIFE-603 K1b), not its wording: the `Folder access` title as a whole line and
+# numbered option rows `1.` and `2.` on consecutive lines, a cursor glyph allowed on either.
+#
+# K1b kept this as a branch of the rule below, gated to the top 20 non-empty lines with a `not` for
+# "a cursor-led row after the option rows". LIFE-603 review r6 showed that region was wrong three ways:
+#   R6-A (fail-closed): a transcript or tool output with the same title + numbered rows inside the top
+#        20 lines, and the composer pushed below them, read as Blocked — the `not` could not see the
+#        composer.
+#   R6-B (fail-open): an old quotation followed by the composer higher up in the same screen, then the
+#        live chooser below it, read as Idle — the `not` matched the old quotation.
+#   R6-C (fail-open): in a pane about 33 columns wide or less, wrapped prose and paths pushed the option
+#        rows past the 20th line and the live chooser read as Idle.
+#
+# What separates the live chooser from everything else is what lies BELOW its option rows. The detection
+# text is the last screenful of the pane (bottom-anchored), and codex draws the chooser alone
+# (codex-rs tui/src/onboarding/trust_directory.rs `render_ref`, identical in 0.162.1, 0.165.0 and main):
+# below the option rows there are only blank rows, an optional red error paragraph and the mandatory
+# `enter continue · esc …` footer (wrapped at narrow widths). A codex screen without the chooser has
+# the composer at its bottom, and the composer's prompt row starts with a prompt glyph. In 0.162.1
+# (`chat_composer.rs` prompt span, `effort_ignition.rs` `prompt_glyph`) that glyph is one of three:
+# `\u{203a}` (default and Max effort), `\u{00bb}` (Ultra effort) or `!` (bash mode) — the same row
+# carries a placeholder, a draft or nothing. Sent user messages in the transcript start with
+# `\u{203a}` too. The glyph is drawn `LIVE_PREFIX_COLS` (2, `ui_consts.rs`) to the left of the text
+# area (`chat_composer.rs` prompt `buf.set_span`), so a prompt row is always the glyph followed by a
+# space or by the end of the row — never by another character. A row is a prompt row here only in that
+# shape: a wrapped path segment or an error row that merely starts with `!`, `\u{00bb}` or `\u{203a}`
+# (`!World/repo`, `!Failed ...` — LIFE-626 review r2 L02~L07) is not one. So this rule looks at the
+# whole screen and matches only when some `Folder access` title is followed by the option pair and,
+# from the pair to the end of the screen, no row is a prompt row (more numbered option rows directly
+# under the pair are allowed — a cursor may sit on option 3). It also requires that no row between the
+# title and the pair is a prompt row: a live chooser has only its path, note and prose there, while a quotation of
+# the chooser followed by a composer whose draft is a numbered list has the quoted cursor row (and
+# usually more prompt-led rows) between the quoted title and the draft's rows.
+#
+# The option rows themselves still accept the wider cursor set `\u{203a} \u{276f} \u{258c} >` the 0.155.1
+# branch uses; that set only widens what counts as an option row. It is NOT the composer test: the
+# 0.162.1 composer never draws `\u{276f}`, `\u{258c}` or `>`, and keeping them there would let a
+# chooser row that merely starts with `>` (an error paragraph, LIFE-626 review r1 A12) hide a live
+# chooser.
+#
+# So a match needs the lowest pair on the screen, which closes all three:
+#   R6-A: the composer row lies below the quoted pair, so nothing matches — whichever of the three
+#         prompt glyphs it shows.
+#   R6-B: the quotation above has the composer below it, but the live pair at the bottom has no
+#         prompt-led row under it, so it matches.
+#   R6-C: there is no line limit, so wrapped prose cannot push the pair out.
+#
+# Both rules share the id `trust_directory` (the id corral maps to reason `trust`) and priority 950. The
+# engine keeps the first matching rule of equal priority, and a screen cannot show both choosers, so
+# their order does not change any state. This rule comes first because `herdr agent explain` previews
+# the region of the first evaluated rule whose id matches: in this order a 0.162.1 chooser previews this
+# rule's whole screen. A 0.155.1 chooser now previews the whole screen too instead of the top 20 lines —
+# cosmetic, and only for the codex version the user no longer runs.
+#
+# Neither the footer nor the path row is required. The path row is not drawn at all when the pane is
+# too low (`cwd_height` is 0) and is centre-truncated when long; the footer adds nothing the rule
+# needs. A composer placeholder (`Ask Codex to do anything`, `Ask a follow-up question`) is not used
+# either: there is more than one, and a composer holding text shows none.
+#
+# Known limits. 1, 2 and 5 need a `Folder access` title line on screen that is not a live chooser and
+# read as Blocked with reason trust (fail-closed); 3 and 4 miss a live chooser (fail-open):
+#   1. a composer DRAFT whose rows form the option pair under a quoted title with no prompt-led row
+#      between them: the draft contains the quoted title and option rows itself, or the draft is a
+#      numbered list of two or more lines under a quotation that has no prompt-led row — a bare title
+#      line, or a quoted plain numbered list without a cursor (LIFE-626 review r1 B05/C01). Below the
+#      pair there is only the status line. A one-line draft that starts like an option row
+#      (`\u{203a} 1. ...`) does not match;
+#   2. a quoted title above a UI that hides the composer: an overlay without prompt-led rows (a working
+#      screen without the composer, review r1 B09), or one that has numbered choices of its own — a
+#      command approval or the model picker (review r1 B06/B08). Its choices form the pair, so a screen
+#      that is Blocked anyway reports reason trust instead of its own;
+#   3. a chooser row that looks exactly like a prompt row — `!`, `\u{00bb}` or `\u{203a}`, then a space or
+#      the row end — hides a live chooser (fail-open). The trust screen draws none of its own: paths
+#      start with `/` or `~` and the prose and footer are fixed English text. Only a wrapped path or
+#      error segment could, when the wrap falls right before such a glyph followed by a space, e.g. a
+#      directory `Hell! World` (review r2 L24);
+#   4. in a pane 22 columns wide or less, option 1 (`\u{203a} 1. Trust and continue`, `Open existing
+#      task`) wraps onto a second row, so the two option rows are no longer consecutive and the live
+#      chooser reads as Idle (fail-open, review r2 L18/L21; `picker_option.rs` wraps the label). The
+#      0.155.1 rule and the LIFE-603 K1b branch had the same requirement — not a regression;
+#   5. a pane that still shows an old chooser frame above the shell line that starts a new codex reads
+#      as Blocked with reason trust until the new codex draws its composer (fail-closed, review r2 S01);
+#      the composer row under it releases it.
 [[rules]]
 id = "trust_directory"
 state = "blocked"
 priority = 950
+region = "whole_recent"
+visible_blocker = true
+regex = ['(?m)^[ \t]*Folder[ \t]+access[ \t]*\r?\n(?:[ \t]*(?:(?:[^ \t\r\n\x{203a}\x{00bb}!]|[\x{203a}\x{00bb}!][^ \t\r\n])[^\r\n]*)?\r?\n)*[ \t]*(?:[\x{203a}\x{276f}\x{258c}>][ \t]+)?1\.[ \t]+[^\r\n]*[^ \t\r\n][ \t]*\r?\n[ \t]*(?:[\x{203a}\x{276f}\x{258c}>][ \t]+)?2\.[ \t]+[^\r\n]*[^ \t\r\n][ \t]*(?:\r?\n[ \t]*(?:[\x{203a}\x{276f}\x{258c}>][ \t]+)?[0-9]+\.[ \t]+[^\r\n]*[^ \t\r\n][ \t]*)*(?:\r?\n[ \t]*(?:(?:[^ \t\r\n\x{203a}\x{00bb}!]|[\x{203a}\x{00bb}!][^ \t\r\n])[^\r\n]*)?)*\z']
+
+[[rules]]
+id = "trust_directory"
+state = "blocked"
+priority = 950
 region = "top_non_empty_lines(20)"
 visible_blocker = true
-all = [
-  { regex = ['\A> You are in [^\r\n]+(?:\r?\n|$)'] },
-  { regex = ['(?s)Do\s+you\s+trust\s+the\s+contents\s+of\s+this\s+directory\?'] },
+# LIFE-394 live captures (codex 0.155.1) showed two reasons this rule could never fire on a real
+# launch, and — once those were fixed — a third reason it fired where it must not:
+#   1. the header is drawn inside a box, so the line is `>-You are in <cwd>---\u{256e}` (U+2500 after `>`),
+#      not `> You are in`;
+#   2. the detection text of a launched pane begins with the shell line that started codex
+#      (`sh-3.2$ codex -m ...`), so `\A` never reached the header at all;
+#   3. with the anchor moved to a line start, a multi-line transcript quote of the same header and
+#      question matched too (review r3 fixture `codex-multiline-quote-idle`).
+# So the rule also requires the live chooser's own control lines — the two numbered options on
+# consecutive rows. Live capture:
+#   >-You are in /private/tmp/.../repo---\u{256e}
+#
+#     Do you trust the contents of this directory? Working with untrusted contents comes with ...
+#
+#   \u{203a} 1. Yes, continue
+#     2. No, quit
+#
+#     Press enter to continue
+#
+# The `not` gate below is what separates a live chooser from a quotation of one. Review r4 showed
+# that a plain "the marker appears anywhere in the region" test is wrong in both directions: a cwd
+# that happens to contain the composer placeholder (`.../ask codex to do anything`) silenced a real
+# chooser. So the gate matches the composer placeholder only as a **whole line** and only **after**
+# the chooser's own option rows — that is the one arrangement a quotation produces and a live
+# chooser cannot.
+#
+# LIFE-603 K1 added the codex-cli 0.162.1 chooser ("Folder access") as a second branch of this rule
+# and K1b widened it from one wording to the screen family. LIFE-626 moved that branch into its own
+# rule above (same id), because this rule's region — the top 20 non-empty lines — was the cause of
+# all three LIFE-603 review r6 findings for it. Only the 0.155.1 branch stays here, unchanged.
+any = [
+  { all = [
+      { regex = ['(?m)^>[ \x{2500}]You are in [^\r\n]+(?:\r?\n|$)'] },
+      { regex = ['(?s)Do\s+you\s+trust\s+the\s+contents\s+of\s+this\s+directory\?'] },
+      { regex = ['(?m)^\s*(?:[\x{203a}\x{276f}\x{258c}>]\s+)?1\.\s+Yes,\s+continue\s*\r?\n\s*(?:[\x{203a}\x{276f}\x{258c}>]\s+)?2\.\s+No,\s+quit\s*$'] },
+    ], not = [
+      { regex = ['(?m)^[ \t]*(?:[\x{203a}\x{276f}\x{258c}>][ \t]+)?2\.[ \t]+No,[ \t]+quit[ \t]*\r?$[\s\S]*?^[ \t]*[\x{203a}\x{276f}\x{258c}>][ \t]+(?i:Ask Codex to do anything)[ \t]*\r?$'] },
+    ] },
 ]
 
